1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:01,576
All right, let's go.

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:02,616
Number 10,

3
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,180
epidemic typhus.

4
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:09,360
The admissions hall of the Sadiki Hospital
in Tunis was an ordinary place in 1909.

5
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,376
Patients arrived in their own clothes,

6
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:12,336
were stripped,

7
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:14,116
bathed with soap and hot water,

8
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,155
and given clean hospital linen.

9
00:00:16,239 --> 00:00:19,080
Their old clothing was bundled
and carried to the laundry.

10
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:20,736
It was routine hygiene,

11
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:23,620
repeated hundreds of times
without a second thought.

12
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,196
But Charles Nicolle,

13
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,976
director of the Pasteur
Institute in Tunis,

14
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:30,640
noticed something strange
about that routine.

15
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,156
Typhus patients infected
the staff who received them,

16
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:36,156
the orderlies who undressed them,

17
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,560
and the workers who washed their clothes.

18
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,476
Once they were bathed and on the ward,

19
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:43,500
they infected no one at all.

20
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,400
The war for Granada was fought
by an army that lived in its clothes.

21
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,856
In the campaigns of 1489 and 1490,

22
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,616
Spanish soldiers slept
in tents and earthworks,

23
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,900
packed shoulder to shoulder for warmth.

24
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:59,620
Blankets passed from the sick
to the well without a second look.

25
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,856
Wool tunics were worn
for weeks without being removed.

26
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:05,796
Linen shirts stiffened
with sweat and wood smoke.

27
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:07,956
At night, the camps
were never fully silent

28
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,400
because thousands of men were scratching.

29
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,276
They scratched at the waistband,

30
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:14,076
the armpits, and the collar

31
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:17,440
until the skin broke
and their fingernails came away dark.

32
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,256
Then, in one tent and then another,

33
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:21,896
the scratching stopped.

34
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:23,536
A man would lie still,

35
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:24,856
staring at nothing,

36
00:01:24,940 --> 00:01:27,920
too far gone and fevered
to feel the itch at all.

37
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:32,160
The cause of that itch was something
every soldier knew and no one feared.

38
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:35,476
The body louse is roughly
the size of a sesame seed.

39
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,296
It does not live on the skin.

40
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:38,916
It lives in the clothing,

41
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,960
clinging to the seams and folds that
sit closest to the body's warmth.

42
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,796
It climbs down to feed
several times a day,

43
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:48,700
then climbs back
into the fabric to digest.

44
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,316
The female lays her eggs along the threads

45
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:55,836
and glues them to the fibers so firmly
that shaking a garment does nothing.

46
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,896
A single unwashed shirt can
shelter hundreds of them.

47
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,416
To a medieval soldier,

48
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,376
lice were as ordinary as mud,

49
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,786
an unpleasant fact
of sleeping in the field.

